; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Divisor Chart
; CATEGORY: Number Theory
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     divisors, primes, grid, patterns, nested loops
; SUMMARY:  Draws a chart with a dot wherever one number divides another, so primes, squares and numbers with many divisors stand out at a glance.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Each row is a number n from 1 to 36. Each column is a possible
;   divisor d. There is a dot where d divides n with nothing left over.
;   The dots for d = 1 and d = n always form the left edge and the
;   diagonal. Rows with only those two dots are the primes; their counts
;   are shown in red at the right. Look down each column too: the
;   column for 3 has a dot in every third row.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change :SIZE to draw a bigger or smaller chart.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * nested FOR loops: one for rows, one for columns
;   * REMAINDER = 0 as the test for "divides"
;   * counting while drawing
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Which rows have an odd number of dots? What kind of numbers are they?
;   * Which number up to 36 has the most divisors?
; ============================================================

to divides :d :n
  output (remainder :n :d) = 0
end

; Screen position of row :n, column :d.
to cx :d
  output -250 + (:d - 1) * :cell
end

to cy :n
  output 250 - (:n - 1) * :cell
end

to row :n
  localmake "found 0
  for [d 1 :n] [
    if divides :d :n [
      make "found :found + 1
      ; the two dots every number has are grey; the others are colored
      ifelse or (:d = 1) (:d = :n) [setpencolor [170 170 180]] [setpencolor item 1 + remainder :d 6 :colors]
      dot (list cx :d cy :n)
    ]
  ]
  ; number at the left, divisor count at the right
  setpencolor [60 60 60]
  penup setxy (cx 1) - 30 (cy :n) - 4 label :n
  setpencolor ifelse :found = 2 [[220 40 40]] [[60 60 60]]
  penup setxy (cx :size) + 20 (cy :n) - 4 label :found
end

to chart
  setpensize 9 setlabelheight 11
  for [n 1 :size] [row :n]
  ; column numbers along the top: 1, 5, 10, 15 ... so they do not crowd
  setpencolor [60 60 60]
  for [d 1 :size] [
    if or (:d = 1) ((remainder :d 5) = 0) [penup setxy (cx :d) - 3 * count :d (cy 1) + 14 label :d]
  ]
end

make "size 36
make "cell 14
make "colors [[230 80 60] [60 120 220] [50 170 90] [240 160 30] [150 80 200] [0 160 170]]

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [252 252 248]
chart
penup setxy 250 264 setpencolor [60 60 60] label [count]
penup setxy -390 280 setlabelheight 16
label [Dots show which numbers divide which]
